            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for the last bus or train, then the Mandarin for to call at a station."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: yōngjǐ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "yōngjǐ — \"crowded\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dìtiě tài yōngjǐ le wǒmen zuò chūzūchē qù — The metro's too crowded: we'll take a taxi."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "yōngjǐ is built from yōng 'to hold close' and jǐ 'to squeeze', both inherited from Middle Chinese, 'jowngX and tsej (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'to hold in the arms' and the second 'to push aside'. Those forms would regularly give yǒng and jī: the tones of yōngjǐ are irregular."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
